  "query_preview": "..\nPer the latest MRMS, the cold front has passed over Ajo to Organ\nPipe and heading east-northeast. Rainfall totals have been\ngenerally 0.1\" to 0.25\" with heavier amounts up to 0.58\" (Organ\nPipe) from rain gauges and radar in the last 3 hours. Weather\nstations only reported gusts up to 23 mph along the front.\nLatest CAMs have been consistent on the placement and the \nmotion of the front. The 18Z HRRR and 15Z RRFS has the front\nslowing down, therefore delaying the arrival of thunderstorms\nand showers in Tucson by an hour. It is now leaning to 3pm to \n4pm for a Tucson arrival. Forecasted rainfall amounts are still\n0.1\"-0.25\" with pockets of 0.5\"-0.75\" from thunderstorms. Wind\ngusts will be 25-30 mph from the front with stronger gusts from\nany thunderstorm up to 45 mph. The highest chances for the\nthunderstorm wind gusts will be now until 5pm, along and near\nI-10 corridor and Tucson Metro. \n\nAfter 5pm, the cold front will pass Tucson and continue east.\nIt will lose energy, leading the thunderstorm line to be \ndisorganize and being more rain showery in nature. Most of the\nactivity will die down after 8pm. There will be lingering rain \nshowers chances through the night and into tomorrow. Best\nchances for rain showers will be north of Tucson as main trough\npasses over the state. \n\nWeather conditions will improve this weekend with Sunday being a\ndryer and warmer day. The upper level pattern will have minor\nridging before the next weather system coming in Monday through\nearly Wednesday. Ensemble models shows the trough moving into\nthe northern half of the state and the best chances for rain\nshowers will north of Tucson. Temperatures will be running\nbelow normal with this second weather system.",
